From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] Optional VIF1/VU1 worker thread with completion at fixed EE points

What it is for:
  A VIF1 DMA kick runs the whole transfer inside the kick: VIF1, every
  VU1 program it starts and their PATH1/PATH2 output to the GIF, before the
  EE's next instruction. On the console VU1 works while the EE goes on.
  In Ben 10: Protector of Earth each frame's two VIF1 transfers take about
  24 ms of game-thread CPU each, and the EE then works about 17 ms before
  it next needs the result; run in parallel that time would overlap.

The change (off unless a port calls setAsyncVif1(true, cycles)):
  - PS2Memory::setAsyncVif1: a VIF1 kick hands the transfer (chains are
    already copied at kick time; direct RAM/scratchpad sources are copied
    now) to a worker thread, which runs processVIF1Data and drains the GIF
    arbiter. The EE goes on.
  - The transfer completes for the EE (D_STAT CIS1, CHCR STR, QWC, the
    DMAC cause; what processPendingTransfers did at once) in syncVif1, on
    the EE thread, after waiting for the worker. It is called at fixed
    points of the EE's execution: an access to DMA channels 1-2, GIF or
    VIF1 registers and FIFOs, a D_STAT read, D_CTRL write, D_ENABLER/W, VU1
    memory, the GS CSR/IMR/SIGLBLID registers; a new VIF1 kick; and in the
    EE scheduler before a VBlank start (so the DMAC handler is queued
    ahead of the VBlank handlers, as with the instant model), when it has
    nothing to run, or after an optional number of EE cycles since the kick
    (EeScheduler::pollAsyncVif1, which then dispatches the DMAC handlers).
    So results depend only on the EE's own progress, not on thread timing. D_STAT and D_PCR writes, timers, INTC,
    VIF0, SIF and scratchpad DMA do not wait.
  - The VU1 MSCAL/MSCNT callbacks read FBRST and report the D/T stop
    through PS2Memory (setVu1StatusHooks): on the worker they use FBRST as
    it was at the kick and the stop reaches VPU_STAT at completion.
  - processPendingTransfers does not drain the GIF arbiter while a
    transfer is in flight (the worker owns it then).
  - PS2Runtime stops the worker after joining the game thread and at the
    start of its destructor, before m_vu1 and m_gs are destroyed.
  With the option off, behaviour is unchanged.

How it was checked:
  See the port's docs/findings/21 s.22: the game runs and renders the
  first level with the option on and off; with it on, the game thread's
  CPU per frame on Linux went from about 76 to 31 ms (the worker 40 ms
  beside it). The replay checksums (VU1 and VU0) and ps2xTest 454/454 are
  unchanged (they do not use the option). The changed files compile with
  MinGW; not yet built with MSVC.

